-- Expected (line, col, len, role) for literal.duo. HAND-WRITTEN from the law.
--
-- What this fixture discriminates: the SPELLING of the string role, and the
-- span boundary. gaps/GAP-078.md ruled the name `literal` — the tracked spec's
-- word — over the projector's `text`; no argument ran either way, so the spec
-- wins on being tracked. Nothing else about the role moved.
--
-- The boundary is the sharper half. §0.8 makes interpolation always available,
-- so a `"…"` is not one opaque span: the DELIMITERS are layout carrying the
-- card `quote`, the CONTENT is `literal`, and a `{hole}` inside reaches the
-- roles in. That is why line 2 is three spans and not one.
--
-- Line 3 is a repair this corpus caught. `'x'` used to fall through to the
-- arithmetic arm and render as operator / place / operator — a string read as
-- two products around a variable. grammar.md §1 gives `string` both spellings.
--
-- The comment on line 1 is the other half of the point. H-6 says the role
-- function is TOTAL over every character, and H-4 names no hue for a comment;
-- it is blue and FAINT, because luminance is surprisal and a comment is
-- inferable. Faint is a statement, never an omission. -- gap[078]
spans = {
    { 1, 1, 10, "comment" },
    { 2, 1, 1, "place" },
    { 2, 3, 1, "operator" },
    { 2, 5, 1, "layout" },
    { 2, 6, 3, "literal" },
    { 2, 9, 1, "layout" },
    { 3, 1, 1, "place" },
    { 3, 3, 1, "operator" },
    { 3, 5, 1, "layout" },
    { 3, 6, 1, "literal" },
    { 3, 7, 1, "layout" },
}
